        <h2>What to Look For When Hiring a Gardener</h2>
        <p>
          Hiring a gardener feels like it should be straightforward — but with the number of people offering garden services in Northampton, it's worth knowing what separates a reliable professional from someone who might let you down or, worse, leave you out of pocket.
        </p>
        <p>Here are the three most important things to check before booking anyone:</p>

        <h3>Public Liability Insurance</h3>
        <p>
          Any professional gardener or garden services company should hold <strong>public liability insurance</strong>. This covers you if something goes wrong — a broken fence panel, a window cracked by a flying stone, or an injury on your property. Without it, you could be liable.
        </p>
        <p>
          Don't be shy about asking to see proof of insurance. A reputable contractor will have it to hand and won't be offended by the question.
        </p>

        <h3>Licensed Waste Carrier Registration</h3>
        <p>
          If the gardener is removing green waste, soil, rubble, or any other material from your property, they must hold a <strong>Waste Carrier Licence</strong> issued by the Environment Agency. Fly-tipping is a serious and growing problem, and if your waste ends up dumped illegally, your name could be connected to it even if someone else physically tipped it.
        </p>
        <p>
          You can check whether a waste carrier is registered on the <strong>Environment Agency's public register</strong> at environment.data.gov.uk. Always ask for the carrier registration number and verify it.
        </p>

        <h3>Reviews and Track Record</h3>
        <p>
          Look for recent reviews — ideally on Google, Facebook, or Checkatrade, where it's harder to fake or cherry-pick feedback. Read the detail of the reviews rather than just the star rating. Good reviews will mention specific aspects like punctuality, tidiness, quality of work, and communication. Be cautious of contractors with few or very old reviews.
        </p>

        <h2>Questions to Ask Before Hiring</h2>
        <p>
          Before confirming any booking, ask:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>Are you fully insured with public liability cover? Can I see the policy?</li>
          <li>Do you hold a waste carrier licence? What's the registration number?</li>
          <li>Will you provide a written quote before starting?</li>
          <li>What does the quote include — specifically, is waste removal included?</li>
          <li>How do you handle unexpected issues or additional work discovered on the day?</li>
          <li>What payment methods do you accept, and when is payment due?</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          A professional contractor will have clear and confident answers to all of these questions.
        </p>

        <h2>Why Local Matters</h2>
        <p>
          A local gardener has reputational skin in the game. They live and work in the same area as their customers — which means they genuinely care about doing a good job. They're also quicker to respond, easier to contact, and can often provide more competitive pricing because they're not travelling long distances.
        </p>
        <p>
          A local gardener also understands the specific conditions of the area — the soil types, the common garden problems, the local weather patterns. In Northamptonshire, that means knowing how clay-heavy soils affect drainage, how to manage the specific hedge varieties common in the county, and how to advise on plants that thrive in the local climate.
        </p>

